From e42dc18dc122227186379531a72edfc7b184910d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dave Seddon <dave.seddon.ca@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 5 Jul 2026 20:38:45 -0700
Subject: [PATCH net-next v1 14/15] net: flow_dissector: descend into FOU/GUE
 inner flow

Opt-in, off by default, byte-identical between the fast and slow paths.
FOU/GUE (Tom Herbert's UDP encapsulations) complete the UDP-tunnel
descent set alongside vxlan/geneve/gtpu.

Unlike VXLAN/Geneve/GTP-U, FOU and GUE have no well-known UDP port, so a
packet cannot be recognised from the wire alone. The dissector consults
the per-netns FOU table, which lives in the tristate fou module: the
built-in dissector reaches it through a registered
struct flow_dissector_fou_ops (registered/unregistered by fou_core.c,
which switches its fou_list to the _rcu list helpers for the lockless
walk). The netns is derived from the skb, so no signature threading; a
raw-buffer dissect (skb == NULL) does not descend.

  - GUE is self-describing: guehdr->proto_ctype gives the inner IP
    protocol. Only version-0 data messages with an IPIP/IPv6 inner are
    handled.
  - Direct FOU is bare -- the inner protocol is not on the wire, it is
    per-tunnel config -- so the port lookup also returns the configured
    inner protocol.

Both descend through the shared flow_dissect_fast_udp_inner() tail
(bounded, ENCAP stamped after the inner pass) on the fast side, and
through the matching branch of __skb_flow_dissect_udp_encap() on the
slow side, so fast == slow holds. Both outer IPv4 and IPv6 are handled.
Covered by the KUnit equivalence suite (with a mock ops provider, so no
real fou config is needed).

Gated by flow_dissector.{gue,fou}_inner, off by default; enabling
changes hashing and depends on the fou module being loaded.

Signed-off-by: Dave Seddon <dave.seddon.ca@gmail.com>
